# Fork `.cursor/` vs upstream PRs

This fork (`seth-wood/zed`) **intentionally commits** `.cursor/skills/` and `.cursor/rules/` so Cloud agents get `verify-zed` and related guidance. Upstream (`zed-industries/zed`) must not receive those files.

## Do

- Treat `.cursor/skills/` and `.cursor/rules/` as **fork-only** infrastructure. Update them on this fork freely.
- Prefer committing skill/rule changes on a fork maintenance branch or `main`, not mixed into an upstream feature PR.
- For any change meant for **upstream**, create the branch from upstream `main`:

  ```bash
  git remote add upstream https://github.com/zed-industries/zed.git 2>/dev/null || true
  git fetch upstream
  git checkout -b <feature-branch> upstream/main
  ```

- Before opening or updating a PR targeting `zed-industries/zed`, run:

  ```bash
  git fetch upstream
  git diff --name-only upstream/main...HEAD | grep -E '^\.cursor/' && exit 1 || true
  ```

  If that prints paths, **stop**: rebase/cherry-pick so `.cursor/` commits are not in the PR range.

## Do not

- Do not open an upstream PR from a branch whose history includes `.cursor/` commits.
- Do not remove `.cursor/` from this fork “to clean a PR”; leave fork skills in place and branch from `upstream/main` instead.
- Do not assume `.gitignore` or `export-ignore` will hide committed `.cursor/` files from a GitHub PR — they will not.

## Quick mental model

| Destination | `.cursor/skills` + `.cursor/rules` |
| --- | --- |
| This fork (`seth-wood/zed`) | Allowed / expected |
| Upstream PR (`zed-industries/zed`) | Forbidden in the PR diff |
